      "content": "To solidify the banking section, consider explaining how modern mortgage underwriting algorithms often implicitly rely on historical neighborhood data to predict creditworthiness, effectively perpetuating redlining patterns without explicitly using the outdated terms. You could also detail the \"interest rate gap\" phenomenon where minority applicants often face higher loan costs, often due to a lack of withered credit history caused by those very same historical exclusionary practices. Furthermore, breaking down how loan-to-value ratios automatically penalize homes in segregated, lower-income neighborhoods shows the mechanical weight of these inequities in current financial institutions. Including specific case studies or recent data trends will ensure the students see the direct line from past policies to present-day wealth disparities.",
